        "summary": {
          "candidates": [
            {
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "locator": "section:lead",
                  "original_text": "是过去一所位于日本爱知县名古屋市天白区的私立短期大学。",
                  "source_ref": "s2"
                },
                {
                  "locator": "section:.E7.AE.80.E4.BB.8B",
                  "original_text": "学校最早可以追溯到1926年即讲习所的创立[注 5]。短期大学为于1950年开办[1]、由学校法人名城大学经营。",
                  "source_ref": "s2"
                },
                {
                  "locator": "section:.E5.8E.86.E5.8F.B2",
                  "original_text": "[编辑]\n1950年 名城大学短期大学部成立并同时设置商经科。分离为两个专攻、以下列举。\n第一部\n第二部[注 3]\n1954年 两个学科成立、以下列举。\n机械科[注 4]\n电机科[注 4]\n1965年 校园迁址至天白区从昭和区。\n2000年 商经科改编为信息国际科。\n2002年 最后一年招生、次年停止招生（正式停办于2005年7月29日[2]）",
                  "source_ref": "s2"
                },
                {
                  "locator": "section:.E6.B3.A8.E9.87.8A",
                  "original_text": "[编辑]\n^ 1.0 1.1 1.2 现在名城大学所在地。\n^ 2.0 2.1 2.2 2.3 改编为信息国际科于2000年。\n^ 3.0 3.1 3.2 招生到1974年、停办于1976年。\n^ 4.0 4.1 4.2 4.3 4.4 4.5 正式停办于1969年。\n^ 日语:名古屋高等理工科講習所\n^ 招生于只信息国际科。",
                  "source_ref": "s2"
                }
              ],
              "id": "c1",
              "value": {
                "language": "en",
                "text": "Meijō Daigaku Tanki Daigakubu (名城大学短期大学部) was a private junior college in Tempaku Ward, Nagoya, Aichi Prefecture, Japan. It was operated by the Meijo University school corporation. The college opened in 1950; the earlier training school to which its history is traced, 名古屋高等理工科講習所, dates to 1926. These dates represent different stages of its institutional history. Its campus moved from Shōwa Ward to Tempaku Ward in 1965.\n\nThe initial commerce and economics department had first and second divisions. Mechanical and electrical departments were added in 1954 and formally closed in 1969. The second division accepted students through 1974 and closed in 1976. In 2000, the commerce and economics department was reorganized as an information and international studies department. The college’s final admissions took place in 2002, limited to that department, and recruitment stopped the following year. The junior college formally closed on 29 July 2005."
              },
              "verification": "supported"
            }
          ],
          "preferred": {
            "candidate_id": "c1",
            "reason_code": "only_available"
          }
        }
